+# Blocked: native async limiter unavailable in vendored `ratelimit`
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`Client.fetch_all_async()` is implemented and `_ThreadedLimiter` is removed, but the
+async tests cannot pass because the vendored `ratelimit` package has no
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()`:
+
+- `vendor/ratelimit.py` is version 2.4.1 and defines only `try_acquire()` and
+  `acquire()`.
+- `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` stops at 2.4.1; it does not mention any async support.
+
+Therefore `asyncio.iscoroutinefunction(RateLimiter.acquire_async)` raises
+`AttributeError` / is false, and calling `await self.limiter.acquire_async()`
+fails the same way.
+
+## Why it cannot be fixed here
+
+`vendor/` is synced from the platform repo (`make vendor`) and must not be edited
+by hand. Adding `acquire_async` locally, monkeypatching it, or restoring the
+threaded/executor shim would all be workarounds that mask a stale vendored
+dependency.
+
+## What is needed
+
+The platform team must cut and publish the `ratelimit` release that contains
+native async support, and this repository must re-run `make vendor` to pull it
+in (2.4.1 is the newest version currently vendored). Once that is done,
+`client.py` and `test_client.py` should work as-is.
+
+## Current test status
+
+- `test_fetch_all_keeps_order` passes.
+- `test_limiter_is_natively_async` and `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`
+  fail until the vendored package is updated.
